    "text": "\n\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n# Implementing a Thread-Safe Bounded LRU Cache in Python\n\n## Table of Contents\n1. [Introduction](#introduction)\n2. [Core Concepts](#core-concepts)\n3. [Step-by-Step Implementation](#step-by-step-implementation)\n4. [Thread Safety](#thread-safety)\n5. [Complete Implementation](#complete-implementation)\n6. [Unit Tests](#unit-tests)\n7. [Performance Considerations](#performance-considerations)\n8. [Usage Examples](#usage-examples)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nAn **LRU (Least Recently Used)** cache is a fixed-size cache that evicts the least recently accessed items when capacity is exceeded. This tutorial walks through building a production-quality LRU cache from scratch, covering:\n\n- **Data structure design** using `OrderedDict` for O(1) operations\n- **Eviction policy** implementation\n- **Thread safety** with proper locking\n- **Comprehensive unit tests**\n\n> **Note:** Python's `functools.lru_cache` exists, but understanding the underlying mechanics is valuable for custom requirements (e.g., TTL, custom eviction, metrics).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n### How LRU Works\n\n```\nCapacity = 3\n\nInsert A \u2192 [A]\nInsert B \u2192 [B, A]        # B is most recent\nInsert C \u2192 [C, B, A]     # C is most recent\nAccess A \u2192 [A, C, B]     # A becomes most recent\nInsert D \u2192 [D, A, C]     # B evicted (least recent)\n```\n\n### Key Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **Bounded** | Fixed maximum number of entries |\n| **LRU Eviction** | Remove least recently used item on overflow |\n| **O(1) Operations** | `get`, `put`, and eviction must be constant time |\n| **Thread Safe** | Safe for concurrent access from multiple threads |\n\n### Why `OrderedDict`?\n\nPython's `collections.OrderedDict` maintains insertion order and provides:\n- `move_to_end(key)` \u2014 O(1) move to end (mark as recently used)\n- `popitem(last=True)` \u2014 O(1) remove last item (evict LRU)\n- Standard",
